<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">John IV, Duke of Brittany</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1339">1339</a>–<a href="/years/1399">1399</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as John of Montfort · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-dreux">House of Dreux</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">John IV, Duke of Brittany, of the House of Montfort, won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-breton-succession">War of the Breton Succession</a> by defeating and killing his rival <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-of-blois">Charles of Blois</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-auray">Battle of Auray</a> in 1364. Recognised as duke by the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerande">Guérande</a> in 1365, he balanced between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and established the Montfort dynasty, which ruled Brittany until 1488.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-iv-duke-of-brittany.jpg" alt="John IV, Duke of Brittany" width="320" height="502" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rs-nourse · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:John_V,_Duke_of_Brittany,_KG.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>John IV, known as John the Conqueror, was the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-of-montfort">John of Montfort</a> and Joanna of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a>. His father claimed the title Duke of Brittany but could enforce the claim only briefly, and only the English king recognised it. For that reason French sources number the son John IV and call the father simply John of Montfort, while English sources have called him John V. After his father's death his mother Joanna continued the war against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-of-blois">Charles of Blois</a> in the name of her baby son and was known as Jeanne la Flamme. She was forced to retreat to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> to ask Edward III for aid, and John and his sister were taken into the king's household.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>John returned to Brittany with English help. In 1364 he won a decisive victory over the House of Blois at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-auray">Battle of Auray</a>, with an English army led by John Chandos, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-of-blois">Charles of Blois</a> was killed. His widow Joanna signed the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerande">Guérande</a> on 12 April 1365, giving up her rights and recognising John as sole master of the duchy. Having won with English support, he had to confirm English barons in strongholds around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brest-france">Brest</a>, which the Breton aristocracy and the French monarchy resented. Although he declared himself a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of Charles V of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, he lost support and went into exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> in 1373.</p><p>Charles V then tried to annex the duchy, sending Bertrand de Guesclin in 1378. The Breton barons revolted and invited John back, and he landed at Dinard in 1379. He reconciled with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-vi-of-france">Charles VI of France</a>, paid off an English army under Thomas of Woodstock and ruled in peace with both crowns for over a decade. He built the Château de l'Hermine at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vannes">Vannes</a> between 1380 and 1385.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1392 an attempt was made in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> on the life of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olivier-de-clisson">Olivier V de Clisson</a>, Constable of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. The attacker, Pierre de Craon, fled to Brittany, and John was assumed to be behind the plot, but Charles VI was seized with madness before he reached the duchy. John was knighted by Edward III into the Order of the Garter between 1375 and 1376, and is believed to be the only Duke of Brittany to receive that honour. He married three times; his third wife, Joan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a>, was the mother of all his children and served as regent for their son John V. He died on 1 November 1399.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “John IV of Brittany” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in the Duchy of Brittany. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nantes">Nantes</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=47.22,-1.55&amp;year=1378" aria-label="Nantes on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4514 507 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of John IV, Duke of Brittany"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4964 777h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4964 777h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=47.22,-1.55&amp;year=1378">What was here in 1378? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
